  "title": "Holly S NDE 4958",
  "date": "1975",
  "content": "A friend dunked my head under the water and I did not know how to hold my breath. I panicked at first, thrashing around trying to find my way up. I then lost all air and the water painfully hit my lungs as I tried to breathe in. The light underwater was blurry and bright but I then I blacked out. The next thing I remember I was hovering about 20 feet over my body, observing. I had been pulled from the water and was lying beside the pool on the concrete slab. Two teenage swimmers, a male and a female, were arguing with each other about what should be done. One wanted to turn me on my side and whack me on the back and the other wanted to do rescue breathing. I did not care about what they were doing, I was just observing. I felt very light and pain free. I wanted to turn and see behind me, but suddenly my body had been turned on its side and I was sucked back into my body instantly. I felt the whacks on my back, water rushing out of my mouth and choking to gain air. I remember sobbing and wailing all the way home, not because of the traumatic experience but because I was sad and angry that I had been pulled back into this painful heavy plane of existence.",
